editorialMd:
Build remaining

Treat `readings` as a row of stamps. Keep a stack, which is a pile where you can add or remove only the top stamp. Read the stamps from left to right. If the next stamp matches the top stamp, remove the top stamp and do not add the new one. Otherwise, put the new stamp on top. `remaining` is the final stack, from bottom to top.

Return the answer

Find the smallest number in `remaining`. Store it in `smallest`. Complete `cancelingStampsFloorTicket` so it returns `smallest`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `remaining` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the input length. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

javascript
function cancelingStampsFloorTicket(readings) {
  const a=readings; let out=[];
  for(const x of a){if(out.length&&out[out.length-1]===x)out.pop();else out.push(x);}
  return out.length?Math.min(...out):0;
}


